## All hands meeting · Sept 29th

A sneak peek of the logo, the colours, the style direction and the moodboards, with the argument behind each. The deck itself is already branded in the new identity, so you're seeing it work rather than a logo on a white page.

## Phase 1 · Strategy

**In progress.** The direction, and the argument for it. What the identity has to say, and to whom.

## Phase 2 · Tone and Voice

**In progress.** How Yardzen sounds.

The Tone and Voice Guide is already underway and the thinking in it is right: brand voice against human voice, split again by whether someone has paid. We finish it, give it a face, and extend it to the places it doesn't reach yet.

## Phase 3 · Visual direction

**Starts Monday.** How Yardzen looks, resolved as a direction. Shown at the all hands on Sept 29th.

This phase explores and chooses. Each item below is worked up as routes, we pick one together, and that one gets expanded. Producing the full set comes after.

| | What we do |
|---|---|
| Direction | Build out the world the brand lives in, as three routes with the argument for each |
| The mark | Explore the logo and the symbol, and land on one |
| Colour and type | Expand the palette and the typefaces, and define the rules for using them |
| Imagery and elements | Define the rules for photography, for when people appear, and for the graphic devices that make a layout read as Yardzen |

## Phase 4 · The brandbook

**Due Oct 30th.** The brand designed out, not summarised: every rule arrives with examples, and the expansion work is designed rather than described. It also lives as a design system online, open to every team and agency, updated as the brand grows.

| | What's in it |
|---|---|
| The brand | Mission, manifesto, personality, and the argument behind the direction |
| Voice | The Tone and Voice Guide, finished and designed in |
| The mark | Lockups, safe areas, minimum sizes, placement, dos and don'ts |
| Colour and type | Values, usage ratios, the rule for deriving new accents, type scale and casing |
| Imagery | Photography principles and treatment, AI artwork, when people appear, video, cropping |
| Elements | Graphic devices, patterns and iconography |
| The grid | Headline zones, CTA placement, contextual type usage |
| Rules for growth | How a sub-brand derives from the master, and how Yardzen sits beside a partner's mark |
| Applied everywhere | The brand designed out across every surface it lives on: website, app, email, social, ads, decks, signage, print |
| Swag and merch | Hats, tees, totes, the box that goes out when a design lands, designed rather than described |
| Campaigns | Worked through per audience: Clients, Contractors, Partners |
| Examples, as many as we can | Every rule shown working, not just stated. A rule with no example is a rule nobody follows |

## Phase 5 · Templates and libraries

**First ship Nov 20th, then something new every week.** Phase 4 designed it. This turns it into something anyone can pick up and use without asking a designer.

| Library | Who it's for | What's in it |
|---|---|---|
| Figma | Designers and the agencies you brief | Components, styles, templates and the grid, linked to the system |
| Canva | Marketing, and anyone who isn't a designer | Brand kit and ready templates, so a post goes out on brand without a designer touching it |
| Digital library | Everyone, including partners | Logos, elements, patterns and approved photography, every format, one link. Platform to be defined |

Templates cover the surfaces you publish on: website, advertising, social, paid media, mailers, partner decks, signage, apparel and swag. Weekly shipping means what you need most arrives first, and you set the order.

## Budget adjustments

| Line | Today | Sep | Oct | Nov | Dec | Jan onward |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Product | $9k | $9k | $9k | $9k | $9k | $9k |
| ENG design system | $3k | $3k | $1k | $1k | $1k | merged |
| Marketing requests | $1k | — | — | — | — | — |
| **Brand** | — | **$3k** | **$7k** | **$7k** | **$7k** | — |
| **Brand + design system** | — | — | — | — | — | **$4k** |
| **Monthly total** | **$13k** | **$15k** | **$17k** | **$17k** | **$17k** | **$13k** |

Product design scope doesn't move, and the day-to-day support stays as it is. Here's how we make that work:

- Casa Soda adds another designer at the end of September, so nothing shifts off product
- The ENG design system runs lean for four months, then merges with brand in January
- A weekly brand review joins the agenda, so decisions get made in the room
- September is $15k, then $17k a month through December. From January it settles back to $13,000, the same as today, with brand and design system running as one line to own and extend what we built

## How we'll know it worked

It starts paying back from October, before it's finished. By the end, here's what's true:

- [ ] Marketing publishes without queuing for a designer
- [ ] An agency gets briefed with a link instead of a call
- [ ] "Is this on brand?" is something you check, not something you ask
- [ ] A new partner or campaign follows a rule instead of starting a project
- [ ] Ad-hoc marketing spend is gone, because the templates replace it
- [ ] Someone who looks Yardzen up finds work as good as the design they heard about

If those aren't true, it didn't work.

## Terms and notes

**Brand work, total.** $24,000 USD. $3k September, then $7k October through December, added to the existing monthly invoice.

**Intellectual property.** Yours, on final payment.

**If it grows or slips.** We agree together what carries into January, rather than either side absorbing it quietly.

## Closing

The Brand Design Brief asks for a distinctive, design-led brand worthy of the landscape work behind it. That ambition is reachable, because the raw material already exists in the projects Yardzen delivers every week. What's missing is the system that presents them to one standard.

This is a living document. We want the budget and the strategy to stay aligned with you rather than fixed in a PDF, so let's discuss it and fine tune it into something that works for all of us.
